---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfToPng from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfToPng.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-png">
  <PdfToPng client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a PDF to PNG</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop a PDF into the box above. pdf.js opens and renders it locally, and the file stays on your device.</li>
      <li>Pick a resolution: 72 DPI for a quick screen-sized copy, 150 DPI for general use, 300 DPI for print or OCR.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Transparent background</strong> if you want the page drawn without a white fill behind it.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Convert to PNG</strong> and watch the gallery fill up page by page.</li>
      <li>Download the whole document as a ZIP, or take individual pages from their thumbnails.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>PNG or JPG?</h2>
    <p>
      Both formats produce a flat picture of the page; they differ entirely in how
      they compress it. PNG is lossless: each pixel is reproduced exactly. JPG
      discards detail that the eye is unlikely to miss in a photograph.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>PNG</th>
            <th>JPG</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Compression</td>
            <td>Lossless</td>
            <td>Lossy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Text and line art</td>
            <td>Perfectly crisp edges</td>
            <td>Visible halos below ~85 quality</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Photographs</td>
            <td>Very large files</td>
            <td>Small files, excellent quality</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Transparency</td>
            <td>Supported (alpha channel)</td>
            <td>Not supported</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Typical A4 page at 150 DPI</td>
            <td>0.5–3 MB</td>
            <td>100–400 KB</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The rule of thumb: choose PNG when the page is a document, a diagram, a
      screenshot or anything with sharp edges you intend to look at closely, and
      <a href="/pdf-to-jpg">JPG</a> when the page is a photograph or when file size
      matters more than the last few percent of fidelity.
    </p>

    <h2>How transparency actually works here</h2>
    <p>
      A PDF page has no background object. What you see as "white paper" is the
      viewer painting white behind the page before drawing anything. When the
      transparent option is on, we skip that fill, so every pixel the page does not
      explicitly draw stays fully transparent in the PNG. That is what you want
      when you need a logo, a signature, a stamp or a diagram to sit on a coloured
      slide.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one important limitation. If the page is a <em>scan</em>, the white
      background is part of the scanned image, not the viewer's background. The
      result will be opaque no matter what this option says, because there is
      genuinely a white photograph covering the page.
    </p>

    <h2>Common uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Design assets: a vector logo or diagram from a PDF at high resolution, with a clean alpha channel.</li>
      <li>Documentation: an exact page image in a wiki, README or knowledge base.</li>
      <li>OCR pipelines: most engines prefer lossless 300 DPI input, and JPG artefacts reduce recognition accuracy.</li>
      <li>Archiving: a pixel-exact record of a page as it looked, independent of future PDF readers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy and limits</h2>
    <p>
      Rendering happens on your device with pdf.js and the browser's own PNG
      encoder, so contracts, medical records and identity documents never touch a
      server. The only real limit is memory: a 300 DPI render of a large-format
      page is a very big bitmap, so the tool automatically reduces the scale rather
      than failing with a blank canvas. Password-protected files must be unlocked
      in your PDF reader first. The tool tells you when that is the problem.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
